   "file": "EpsilonEridani/Relativity/Tensors/RealTensor/Vector/MinkowskiProductExtensions.lean",
   "fix": "Swap explicitness to `(w : Vector d) {n : Vector d}` so `w.minkowskiProduct_self_nonpos_of_orthogonal_causal hn hn0 hnw` names its own subject (`n` is determined by unification from `hn`/`hnw`), and update the single use in `GPD/Kinematics.lean:303` to `K.deltaOrthN.\u2026`. Optionally reorder the suffix to `_of_causal_of_orthogonal`.",
   "issue": "`minkowskiProduct_self_nonpos_of_orthogonal_causal` makes `n` the first explicit argument (`(n : Vector d) {w : Vector d}`) while the conclusion `\u27eaw, w\u27eb\u2098 \u2264 0` is about `w`. Dot notation therefore misstates the lemma at the call site: `K.n.minkowskiProduct_self_nonpos_of_orthogonal_causal \u2026` (Kinematics.lean:303) reads as `\u27ean, n\u27eb\u2098 \u2264 0`, the opposite of hypothesis `hn : 0 \u2264 \u27ean, n\u27eb\u2098`. The `of_`-clause also reverses hypothesis order (`hn` causal, then `hn0`, then `hnw` orthogonal), against the Mathlib rule that hypotheses are listed in the order they appear.",
   "line": 181
  }
 ],
